<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vix Grave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 470 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vix Grave is a burial mound at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-lassois">Mont Lassois</a>, near Vix in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, where a person of high rank, usually identified as a woman, was buried about 470 BCE. Excavated undisturbed in 1953, it held a gold torc and imported vessels for wine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the Greek world. Chief among them is the Vix krater, a bronze vessel 1.64 metres high, the largest known metal vessel from western classical antiquity. The krater and a reconstruction of the grave are in the museum at Châtillon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vix-grave.jpg" alt="Vix Grave" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christophe Finot · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chatillon-sur-Seine_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Pays_chatillonnais_-_Crat%C3%A8re_de_Vix_-_01_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The grave lies near the village of Vix, about 6 km north of Châtillon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Côte-d'Or. It belongs to a Celtic complex of the Late Hallstatt and Early La Tène periods centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-lassois">Mont Lassois</a>, a steep, flat-topped hill crowned by a fortified settlement with ditches and walls up to 8 m thick. In the sixth and fifth centuries BCE the settlement appears to have controlled a trading point where the Seine crossed the land route from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 2006 a hall measuring 35 by 22 m, likened to an early Greek megaron, was found at its centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Maurice Moisson found the mound, which he and René Joffroy excavated early in 1953. Under a tumulus of earth and stone once 40 m across and 6 m high, a wooden chamber 3 m square held a body laid in the box of a cart whose wheels had been taken off and set beside it. She was between 35 and 40 years old at death. Her jewellery included a 480-gram gold torc, fibulae and bracelets of slate and amber. Drinking cups from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> lay with the krater, and one dated to about 525 BCE gives the earliest date the burial can have.</p><p>The krater weighs 208.6 kilograms, holds 1,100 litres and was made of seven or more pieces bearing Greek letters, so it was probably carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in parts and assembled there; it may have been made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sybaris">Sybaris</a>. Its neck carries a frieze of eight four-horse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, each followed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a>, and its lid a bronze statuette of a veiled woman. Some scholars link it to gift exchange between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> wine traders and northern elites. The identification of the dead as a woman has been questioned since a reexamination by Bettina Arnold in 1991, and an osteological analysis by Knüsel in 2002 concluded that the individual was likely female, with some androgynous features.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Research on and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-lassois">Mont Lassois</a> resumed in 1991 under Bruno Chaume, and the French National Institute for Preventive Archaeological Research reopened the mound in 2019. A reconstruction of the grave and the original finds are displayed in the museum at Châtillon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, from which the gold torc was stolen on 24 July 2026.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vix Grave” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Greek bronze krater, a vessel for mixing wine, found in a princely grave at <b>Vix</b> in Burgundy stands 1.64 metres high, weighs 208.6 kilograms and holds about 1,100 litres: the largest Greek bronze vessel known.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Massalia (Marseille) drew trade up the Rhône and Saône, and early La Tène elite burials such as the <b>Vix Grave</b> in Burgundy hold imported luxuries; by 500 BCE, as the Etruscans came to border the Celts in northern Italy, trade across the Alps overtook the Rhône route.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
